@page "/docs"
<PageTitle>Introduction - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-3">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Introduction</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Blazor</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">Tailwind v4</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">.NET 10</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">Blaizio is a component system for Blazor built as <b>two layers with one rule</b>: behavior ships as a package, and styling lives in your source. The headless primitives take care of accessibility, keyboard support, focus and state. The styled components are Tailwind files that the CLI copies <em>into your project</em>, where you can open, read and change every line.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">The two layers</h2>
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
            <div class="rounded-xl border bg-card p-5 text-card-foreground">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                    <div class="text-sm font-semibold">Blaizio.Base</div>
                    <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">NuGet</BzBadge>
                </div>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Headless primitives that render the correct elements with ARIA roles, keyboard and focus behavior, and a <code>data-state</code> attribute contract. They ship no CSS, so you can style them with anything or skip the styled layer entirely. <a class="font-medium text-foreground underline underline-offset-4" href="docs/base">Read the Base guide</a>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl border bg-card p-5 text-card-foreground">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                    <div class="text-sm font-semibold">Styled components</div>
                    <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">your source</BzBadge>
                </div>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Tailwind-styled <code>Bz*</code> components that <a class="font-medium text-foreground underline underline-offset-4" href="docs/cli">the CLI</a> copies into your project, rewritten to your namespace and tracked for <code>add --diff</code> and <code>update</code>. Nothing is hidden inside a package. Eight styles, color presets, dark mode and RTL are included.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Quick start</h2>
        <p>Install the CLI once with <CmdChip Command="dotnet tool install -g Blaizio.Cli" />, then run this in the folder that holds your Blazor project's <code>.csproj</code>:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Installation/InitExisting" />
        <p>That single command gives you a working setup. It installs the packages, writes the tokens file (<code>Styles/app.css</code>), wires the Tailwind pipeline (the default <b>standalone</b> mode needs no Node), patches your host page and copies four components, plus their dependencies, into your source tree. To check it, run <code>dotnet build</code> and confirm that <code>wwwroot/app.css</code> exists and is not empty.</p>
        <p>The <a href="docs/installation">Installation page</a> walks through new apps, existing apps and Base-only setups in full.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">What you get</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>60+ components</b>: from buttons to combo boxes, data tables, command palettes, charts and a sortable tree. Every page has a copy-paste usage block and a live demo.</li>
            <li><b>Accessibility as a baseline</b>: roles, keyboard behavior and focus management live in the primitive, so they survive any restyling you do.</li>
            <li><b>Theming that composes</b>: eight styles (inlined into the components you install), color presets, chart palettes, radius scales, real webfonts and dark mode. Your whole look lives in one tokens file plus your component source, and you can preview it live on <a href="themes">Themes</a>.</li>
            <li><b>A real CLI</b>: <code>new</code>, <code>add</code> (with <code>--diff</code>), <code>update</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>remove</code> and <code>uninstall</code>. Every change is tracked in <code>blaizio.json</code>, so <code>uninstall</code> removes exactly what was added.</li>
            <li><b>RTL out of the box</b>: the components use logical properties throughout, so they mirror as soon as you set <code>dir="rtl"</code>. See <a href="docs/direction">Direction</a>.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Where next</h2>
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
            <a href="docs/installation" class="rounded-xl border bg-card p-5 text-card-foreground transition-colors hover:border-primary/50">
                <div class="text-sm font-semibold">Installation</div>
                <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Prerequisites and both setup paths, step by step.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="docs/components" class="rounded-xl border bg-card p-5 text-card-foreground transition-colors hover:border-primary/50">
                <div class="text-sm font-semibold">Components</div>
                <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Demos, usage and the API reference for every component.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="themes" class="rounded-xl border bg-card p-5 text-card-foreground transition-colors hover:border-primary/50">
                <div class="text-sm font-semibold">Themes</div>
                <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Compose a theme live and ship it as one command.</p>
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
